      "pronunciationText": "To find those fractions, we need to know how heavy each kind of atom is. Atoms are far too light to weigh one at a time, so chemists compare them on one scale, called relative atomic mass. On that scale, a carbon atom is twelve point zero and an oxygen atom is sixteen point zero. They're just comparison numbers, with no units, and you'll find them on the periodic table or in the question. A later video explains why many of them aren't whole numbers.",
