      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "It's more, and here's why. Add up the whole molecule: one carbon at twelve point zero, plus two oxygens at sixteen point zero each. That's forty-four point zero, the relative formula mass. The oxygen's share is two times sixteen point zero, which is thirty-two point zero. So oxygen is thirty-two point zero over forty-four point zero, times a hundred, which is seventy-two point seven percent. That's more than two thirds, because each oxygen atom is heavier than the carbon atom. Counting atoms isn't the same as weighing them.",
